# Domains

This folder is your vertical's home. Organise all domain feature modules here.

## Structure

Each domain module lives in its own sub-folder:

```
Domains/
└── YourFeature/
    ├── Controllers/    ← HTTP endpoints for this feature
    ├── Services/       ← Business logic and orchestration
    ├── Data/           ← EF Core entities and their IEntityTypeConfiguration classes
    ├── Models/         ← Request / response records for this feature
    ├── Validators/     ← FluentValidation validators for this feature's request models
    └── Exceptions/     ← Domain exceptions specific to this feature
```

## Rules

- One folder per domain feature — never mix two features in the same folder.
- Controllers handle HTTP concerns only — no business logic.
- Services contain business logic — no `HttpContext`, no direct EF Core.
- Validators live alongside the request models they validate.
- Domain exceptions are caught at the controller boundary — not in global middleware.
- Register all services in `Extensions/ServiceCollectionExtensions.cs`.
{{- if model.manifest.database != null }}
- One `DbContext` for the whole service: `Data/{{ model.names.dot_net_class_name }}DbContext.cs`. Add an `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` class next to each entity; it is picked up automatically. Never create a feature `DbContext`.
{{- end }}
{{- if model.manifest.tenant_ready }}

## Tenancy (read before you store any data)

This service keeps each customer's data apart. Your entities must be tenant-owned, and the rules are short:

- Derive entities from `TenantEntity<Guid>` (or mark shared data `[SharedEntity]`).
- Never put `TenantId` in a request, route or query string. The platform knows the tenant.
- Use `IRepository<TEntity, Guid>` and `IUnitOfWork`; another tenant's row is simply not found (404).
- Wrap background work in `ITenantScopeFactory.BeginScope(tenantId)`.

Start with [`DeveloperReference/TenancyQuickStart.md`](../../../DeveloperReference/TenancyQuickStart.md): a complete feature in six steps and the checklist for every pull request.
{{- end }}

## Example

```
Domains/
├── Billing/
│   ├── Controllers/
│   │   └── BillingController.cs
│   ├── Services/
│   │   ├── IBillingService.cs
│   │   └── BillingService.cs
│   ├── Data/
│   │   ├── BillingEntity.cs
│   │   └── BillingEntityConfiguration.cs
│   ├── Models/
│   │   ├── CreateBillingRequest.cs
│   │   └── BillingResponse.cs
│   ├── Validators/
│   │   └── CreateBillingRequestValidator.cs
│   └── Exceptions/
│       ├── BillingNotFoundException.cs
│       └── DuplicateBillingException.cs
└── Pricing/
    ├── Controllers/
    │   └── PricingController.cs
    ├── Services/
    │   ├── IPricingService.cs
    │   └── PricingService.cs
    ├── Models/
    │   ├── UpdatePricingRequest.cs
    │   └── PricingResponse.cs
    └── Exceptions/
        └── PricingNotFoundException.cs
```

## Common Utilities

The `Common/` folder at the project root contains shared utilities available to all domain modules:

- `PagedResult<T>` — standard paginated response wrapper
- `WorkbookReader` — `.xlsx` file parsing utility
- `DeduplicationHelper` — SHA-256 content hash for duplicate file detection
- `ConfirmationPhraseValidator` — typed validation for destructive operation confirmation phrases

See `Common/` for usage examples in the XML doc comments.

